@@ -239,6 +239,25 @@ prettyBytes(100_000, {fixedWidth: 10});
 [1000, 10_000, 100_000].map(bytes => prettyBytes(bytes, {fixedWidth: 8}));
 //=> ['    1 kB', '   10 kB', '  100 kB']
 ```
+### parseBytes(string)
+
+Parse a size string into a number of bytes. It is the reverse of `prettyBytes(number, {binary: true})`, but not of the default decimal output, since `kB` here means 1024 bytes.
+
+Supports plain numbers and the units `B`, `KiB`, `MiB`, `GiB`, `TiB`, `PiB`, `EiB`, `ZiB`, and `YiB`. The units without the `i` (`kB` or `KB`, `MB`, `GB`, `TB`, `PB`, `EB`, `ZB`, and `YB`) are also accepted and are read as powers of 1024, like Windows Explorer does, so `'1 MB'` is `1048576`, not `1000000`. Units are case-sensitive and fractional values are allowed. Other input that cannot be parsed, including other casings like `kb`, throws a `SyntaxError`.
+
+```js
+import {parseBytes} from 'pretty-bytes';
+
+parseBytes('512');
+//=> 512
+
+parseBytes('1.5 KiB');
+//=> 1536
+
+parseBytes('1 MB');
+//=> 1048576
+```
+
 
